OpenCloud on FreeBSD
====================

This file describes the FreeBSD specific parts of running OpenCloud. For
everything else see the upstream documentation: https://docs.opencloud.eu/

Paths used by this port:

  binary                /usr/local/bin/opencloud
  configuration         /usr/local/etc/opencloud/
  environment file      /usr/local/etc/opencloud/opencloud.env
  data directory        /var/db/opencloud/
  log file              /var/log/opencloud.log
  log rotation          /usr/local/etc/newsyslog.conf.d/opencloud.conf
  service user          opencloud

The data and configuration directories are compiled into the binary. Do not
set HOME, OC_BASE_DATA_PATH or OC_CONFIG_DIR unless you want to move them.


1. First start
--------------

  # service opencloud init

This creates the initial configuration and prints the password of the
"admin" user once. Write it down.

The package installs /usr/local/etc/opencloud/opencloud.env from the sample
file opencloud.env.sample if it does not exist yet. Edit it and adjust at
least OC_URL, then enable and start the service:

  # sysrc opencloud_enable=YES
  # service opencloud start

OC_URL must be the exact public URL users will type, including the scheme.
Use https.


2. Storage
----------

The user data is stored below /var/db/opencloud/storage/users. The default
storage driver ("posix") keeps all metadata in extended attributes of the
files themselves, there is no database. Requirements from upstream:

  * The filesystem must support extended attributes.
  * The whole tree below storage/users must be on ONE filesystem. Moving
    files between filesystems is not supported.
  * All files and directories must belong to the service user (opencloud).
  * Nothing but OpenCloud may modify the tree while the service runs.
    Stop the service before changing anything from the outside.

Because of this it is recommended to give the data its own filesystem
(on ZFS: its own dataset), so that snapshots, quotas and the properties
below apply to the OpenCloud data only. On ZFS create the dataset with
system attributes enabled BEFORE the first start of the service:

  # zfs create -o mountpoint=/var/db/opencloud <pool>/opencloud
  # zfs set xattr=sa <pool>/opencloud

If the dataset was created before the package was installed, make sure the
service user owns it:

  # chown opencloud:opencloud /var/db/opencloud
  # chmod 0750 /var/db/opencloud

Upstream documentation:
  https://docs.opencloud.eu/docs/admin/getting-started/requirements/
  https://docs.opencloud.eu/docs/admin/configuration/storage/storage-posix/


3. Running behind a reverse proxy
---------------------------------

OpenCloud has a built-in HTTP(S) proxy that serves the web interface, the
WebDAV and the API endpoints on one port (9200 by default). It is meant to
sit behind a web server that terminates TLS and serves one dedicated
host name, for example cloud.example.com, so the examples below use a
dedicated sub-domain.

Settings in /usr/local/etc/opencloud/opencloud.env when the web server runs
on the same host and terminates TLS:

  # The public URL, must match the host name of the virtual host below.
  OC_URL=https://cloud.example.com

  # Listen on the loopback interface only, the web server is the only client.
  PROXY_HTTP_ADDR=127.0.0.1:9200

  # TLS is terminated by the web server. Without this OpenCloud expects
  # TLS and a certificate on its own port.
  PROXY_TLS=false

Restart the service after changes:  # service opencloud restart

Make sure that port 9200 is not reachable from the outside (firewall or the
loopback address above). Basic authentication should stay disabled in
production, leave PROXY_ENABLE_BASIC_AUTH unset unless a client needs it.

3.1 Caddy (www/caddy)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Caddy obtains and renews the certificate by itself (the host name must
resolve to the server and ports 80 and 443 must be reachable).
/usr/local/etc/caddy/Caddyfile:

  cloud.example.com {
    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:9200
  }

  # sysrc caddy_enable=YES
  # service caddy start

Caddy passes the Host header and sets the X-Forwarded-* headers itself and
does not limit the size of uploads.

3.2 Apache (www/apache24)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Load the modules in httpd.conf (most are already there, check for
duplicates):

  LoadModule socache_shmcb_module libexec/apache24/mod_socache_shmcb.so
  LoadModule ssl_module           libexec/apache24/mod_ssl.so
  LoadModule headers_module       libexec/apache24/mod_headers.so
  LoadModule proxy_module         libexec/apache24/mod_proxy.so
  LoadModule proxy_http_module    libexec/apache24/mod_proxy_http.so

A virtual host, for example in /usr/local/etc/apache24/Includes/cloud.conf:

  <VirtualHost *:80>
    ServerName cloud.example.com
    Redirect permanent / https://cloud.example.com/
  </VirtualHost>

  <VirtualHost *:443>
    ServerName cloud.example.com

    SSLEngine on
    SSLCertificateFile    /usr/local/etc/ssl/cloud.example.com/fullchain.pem
    SSLCertificateKeyFile /usr/local/etc/ssl/cloud.example.com/privkey.pem

    # OC_URL must match the host name the client used.
    ProxyPreserveHost On
    RequestHeader set X-Forwarded-Proto "https"

    # Keep encoded characters in WebDAV paths intact.
    AllowEncodedSlashes NoDecode
    ProxyPass        / http://127.0.0.1:9200/ nocanon
    ProxyPassReverse / http://127.0.0.1:9200/
  </VirtualHost>

  # service apache24 configtest
  # service apache24 reload

If OpenCloud keeps its own TLS (PROXY_TLS left at its default) use
"SSLProxyEngine on" and https://127.0.0.1:9200/ instead. The certificate of
OpenCloud is self-signed in that case, so Apache has to be told not to
verify it, which is why terminating TLS only in Apache is preferred.


4. Upgrades and configuration changes
-------------------------------------

Part of the settings (secrets, service passwords, the layout of the
internal services) is kept in /usr/local/etc/opencloud/opencloud.yaml, which
is created once by "service opencloud init". A new release may need
additional entries. After an upgrade, or after changing OC_* variables in
opencloud.env, check it:

  # service opencloud diffconfig

This runs "opencloud init --diff" as the service user. The existing
secrets, passwords and IDs are kept, so only new or removed settings show up
as differences. opencloud.yaml is not changed. If there are differences they
are printed and also written to
/usr/local/etc/opencloud/opencloud.config.patch; otherwise it prints "no
changes, your config is up to date". After reviewing the patch it can be
applied as the service user, so that the file keeps its owner:

  # cd /usr/local/etc/opencloud
  # su -m opencloud -c 'patch -b opencloud.yaml opencloud.config.patch'
  # service opencloud restart

  # service opencloud reconfig

runs "opencloud init --force-overwrite". OpenCloud saves the old file as
opencloud.yaml.<date>.backup first. WARNING: the new file contains NEW
secrets and service passwords, and the internal services of an existing
instance were set up with the old ones, so this can lock them out. Use it on
a fresh instance, or after wiping the data directory. To go back, restore
the backup and restart the service.


5. Log rotation
---------------

The service writes its output to /var/log/opencloud.log. The package
installs /usr/local/etc/newsyslog.conf.d/opencloud.conf, which keeps seven
compressed files and rotates daily at midnight. The service is started with
daemon -H, so it reopens the log file when newsyslog sends it SIGHUP; no
restart is needed. Adjust the file for another schedule, or set
opencloud_logfile in rc.conf and change the name in both places.

OC_LOG_LEVEL in opencloud.env controls how much is logged (default in the
sample file: error).


6. Running opencloud commands (option CLI)
------------------------------------------

The opencloud binary is also the administration tool (opencloud version,
opencloud posixfs consistency, opencloud shares cleanup, ...). Running it as
root creates files owned by root in the data directory, which the posix
storage driver does not support, and ignores the variables from
opencloud.env.

If the port was built with the CLI option (off by default) the script

  # opencloud-cli <arguments>

runs opencloud as the service user with the settings from opencloud.env,
for example:

  # opencloud-cli version
  # opencloud-cli posixfs consistency /var/db/opencloud/storage/users

Without the option, load the environment file in a Bourne shell and use
su(8), which keeps the environment with -m:

  # set -a; . /usr/local/etc/opencloud/opencloud.env; set +a
  # su -m opencloud -c '/usr/local/bin/opencloud version'
